## Abstract

We evaluate the citation accuracy of three frontier language models—DeepSeek, Claude, and ChatGPT—when forced to provide formal academic references in response to research questions. Across 15 responses and 45 citations spanning three domains (CRISPR therapies, gut microbiome, and high-temperature superconductivity), we find that 71% of citations were real and accurate, 27% were real but distorted (primarily author misattribution), and 2% were fully fabricated. The dominant failure mode is not fabrication of non-existent papers but distortion of existing papers, most commonly by attributing them to incorrect authors. Model performance varied: Claude achieved 80% accuracy, ChatGPT 73% (with one fabrication), and DeepSeek 60%. These findings suggest that research agents must implement author-level verification, not merely DOI resolution, to ensure citation integrity.

---

## 1. Introduction

Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed as research assistants, literature review tools, and autonomous research agents. In these roles, they are often expected to provide citations to support their claims. The integrity of these citations is critical: fabricated or distorted references can mislead researchers, propagate misinformation, and undermine trust in AI-assisted research.

Prior work has documented that LLMs can hallucinate references—inventing papers that do not exist (Walters & Wilder, 2023; Bhattacharyya et al., 2023). However, most studies focus on fully fabricated citations. Less is known about *distorted* citations: real papers that are misattributed to wrong authors, assigned incorrect years, or published in wrong venues. These distortions are arguably more dangerous because they survive superficial verification—a DOI or title search will confirm the paper exists, but the researcher may not notice that the authors are wrong.

In this paper, we present a small-scale but systematic evaluation of citation integrity in three frontier models: DeepSeek, Claude, and ChatGPT. We force the models to provide formal academic references in a strict format, then manually verify every citation against authoritative sources. Our goal is to characterize the *types* of citation errors that occur, not just their frequency.

We find that fabrication is rare (2%), but distortion is common (27%). The dominant failure mode is **author misattribution**: citing a real paper but crediting it to the wrong authors. This has direct implications for the design of research agents, which currently rely on DOI resolution or title matching for citation verification.

---

## 2. Related Work

Hallucination in LLMs has been extensively studied (Ji et al., 2023). In the context of citation generation, several studies have reported high rates of fabricated references. Walters & Wilder (2023) found that ChatGPT generated fabricated citations in response to academic queries. Bhattacharyya et al. (2023) reported that a majority of ChatGPT-generated medical references were fabricated or inaccurate.

Attribution and faithfulness in natural language generation have also been explored (Maynez et al., 2020; Rashkin et al., 2021; Bohnet et al., 2022). These works focus on whether generated text is supported by cited sources, but they do not typically evaluate the accuracy of the citation metadata itself.

Our work contributes a fine-grained analysis of citation errors, distinguishing between fabrication, distortion, and accuracy. We also evaluate multiple models and multiple domains, providing a comparative perspective.

