## 3. Methodology

### 3.1 Models

We evaluated three frontier models:

- **DeepSeek** (version unspecified; accessed via web interface)
- **Claude** (version unspecified; accessed via web interface)
- **ChatGPT** (version unspecified; accessed via web interface)

All models were accessed in September 2026. Exact version numbers were not recorded, which is a limitation of this study.

### 3.2 Questions

We designed five research questions across three domains:

1. How effective are constitutional AI methods at reducing harmful outputs in large language models? (AI/ML)
2. What are the main limitations of current retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) systems? (AI/ML)
3. What is the current evidence on CRISPR-based therapies for sickle cell disease? (Biology/Medicine)
4. How does the gut microbiome influence neurodegenerative diseases such as Parkinson's and Alzheimer's? (Biology/Medicine)
5. What is the current status of research on high-temperature superconductivity in hydrogen-rich materials? (Physics)

Questions 1 and 2 were pilot questions. The models did not provide formal citations in response to these questions even when asked, so they were excluded from the citation analysis. Questions 3–5 form the core dataset.

### 3.3 Prompt Design

For Questions 3–5, we used the following strict prompt:

> Answer the following research question and provide 4–5 academic citations.
>
> You MUST include a separate "References" section.
> Each reference must follow exactly:
>
> Authors. (Year). Title. Venue. DOI/URL (if available).
>
> Do not just describe studies — list the actual papers.
>
> Question: [QUESTION]

### 3.4 Verification Process

Every citation produced by the models was manually verified using the following sources: Google Scholar, PubMed, DOI resolution (doi.org), and publisher websites (NEJM, Nature, Science, etc.). We checked whether the paper exists, whether the title is correct, whether the authors are correct, whether the year is correct, whether the venue is correct, and whether the DOI resolves to the correct paper.

### 3.5 Scoring Categories

Each citation was classified into one of three categories:

- **Real & Accurate** — Paper exists and all metadata (authors, year, title, venue) are correct.
- **Real but Distorted** — Paper exists, but at least one metadata field is incorrect.
- **Fully Fabricated** — No evidence that the paper exists.

