## 4. Results

### 4.1 Overall Citation Accuracy

Across Questions 3–5, the three models produced a total of 45 citations.

| Category | Count | Percentage |
|----------|-------|------------|
| Real & Accurate | 32 | 71% |
| Real but Distorted | 12 | 27% |
| Fully Fabricated | 1 | 2% |

Fabrication was rare. Distortion was the dominant failure mode.

### 4.2 Per-Model Performance

| Model | Total | Accurate | Distorted | Fabricated | Accuracy Rate |
|-------|-------|----------|-----------|------------|---------------|
| DeepSeek | 15 | 9 | 6 | 0 | 60% |
| Claude | 15 | 12 | 3 | 0 | 80% |
| ChatGPT | 15 | 11 | 3 | 1 | 73% |

Claude was the most accurate. ChatGPT produced the only fabrication. DeepSeek had the highest distortion rate.

### 4.3 Per-Question Analysis

| Question | Model | Citations | Accurate | Distorted | Fabricated |
|----------|-------|-----------|----------|-----------|------------|
| Q3 (CRISPR) | DeepSeek | 5 | 5 | 0 | 0 |
| Q3 (CRISPR) | Claude | 5 | 5 | 0 | 0 |
| Q3 (CRISPR) | ChatGPT | 5 | 4 | 1 | 0 |
| Q4 (Microbiome) | DeepSeek | 5 | 1 | 4 | 0 |
| Q4 (Microbiome) | Claude | 5 | 3 | 2 | 0 |
| Q4 (Microbiome) | ChatGPT | 5 | 4 | 1 | 0 |
| Q5 (Superconductivity) | DeepSeek | 5 | 3 | 2 | 0 |
| Q5 (Superconductivity) | Claude | 5 | 4 | 1 | 0 |
| Q5 (Superconductivity) | ChatGPT | 5 | 3 | 1 | 1 |

The CRISPR question produced the most accurate citations. The microbiome and superconductivity questions produced more distortions. The only fabrication occurred on the superconductivity question, which was the hardest and most niche topic.

### 4.4 Types of Distortions

Of the 12 distorted citations, the breakdown by error type was:

| Error Type | Count | Example |
|------------|-------|---------|
| Wrong authors | 9 | DeepSeek credited a real paper to "Ashraf, G. M." instead of actual authors. |
| Wrong venue | 2 | ChatGPT cited Zarghamian et al. in *HemaSphere* (actual: *Frontiers in Medicine*). |
| Wrong title | 1 | DeepSeek used the title of a different paper for a real DOI. |
| Wrong year/volume/DOI | 1 | Claude cited Hirsch & Marsiglio (2022) in *Phys. Rev. B* 106, 214511 — actual paper is *Phys. Rev. B* 103, 134505 (2021). |

Author misattribution was by far the most common distortion.

### 4.5 Case Study: First Fabrication

On Question 5, ChatGPT produced the following citation:

> Bianconi, A., & Jarlborg, T. (2021). Superconductivity above 100 K in MgB₂-type structures and in hydrogen-rich compounds. *Journal of Superconductivity and Novel Magnetism*. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10948-021-05995-0

This paper does not exist. The DOI does not resolve. Bianconi and Jarlborg have published together on related topics (e.g., *EPL* 112, 37001, 2015; *J. Supercond. Nov. Magn.* 31, 689–695, 2018), but not this paper, not in this journal, and not in 2021. This is a fully fabricated citation.

Notably, this fabrication occurred on the hardest question in the set—the one requiring synthesis of experimental, theoretical, and reproducibility issues in a niche field.

